# Phase 004 causal epistemics rules

Status: **Groups 01–04 accepted — REF-001–REF-030. Group 05 next and not started.**

Preserve:

- causal candidate/proposal ≠ support ≠ confirmation;
- leading hypothesis ≠ confirmed cause;
- unresolved ≠ unevaluated/proposed;
- rejected ≠ unsupported/lower ranked;
- causal contribution ≠ primary cause ≠ percentage attribution;
- one supported contributor ≠ exclusion of compatible contributors;
- Investigation closure ≠ confirmation;
- confirmation evidence sufficiency ≠ confirmation authority;
- human title/role ≠ automatic confirmation authority;
- automated reasoning capability ≠ automatic confirmation authority;
- current confirmed status ≠ immutable historical truth;
- direct control-mechanism evidence ≠ automatic confirmation of a broader consequence.

Rules:

- Bind every material Causal Claim to a proposed cause, defined outcome, causal role, subject/context/time, and material mechanism/transmission assumptions.
- Use `proposed`, `supported`, `weakened`, `unresolved`, `rejected`, `confirmed`.
- Preserve non-linear historical status transitions with knowledge/evaluation time.
- `Supported` requires applicable material support below confirmation.
- `Unresolved` means substantive evaluation occurred but evidence remains insufficient/conflicting/non-discriminating/unavailable/restricted.
- `Rejected` requires sufficient contradiction/exclusion evidence; lack of support alone is insufficient.
- Evaluate applicable cause/effect occurrence, temporal ordering, relationship/mechanism, encounter/transmission, semantic direction, contrasts/interventions, material alternatives, and exclusion coverage.
- Do not create a universal causal confidence score.
- Maintain a bounded material alternative set; compatible contributors may coexist.
- `Primary` requires comparative evidence; qualitative roles do not imply percentage attribution.
- `Confirmed` requires an explicit claim-class confirmation profile plus bound proposition, sufficient cause/effect evidence, required ordering/mechanism/transmission conditions, contradiction/alternative review, adequate exclusion coverage, named/versioned standard, resolved confirmation authority/capability, and provenance-bearing confirmation action.
- Phase 004 does not decide which actor/system may confirm; roles, ownership, admin status, service identity, or automation do not self-authorize.
- Automated confirmation is allowed only if later accepted semantics explicitly permit it and evidence/authorization conditions are satisfied.
- RCA can mature progressively; speed and elapsed time do not inflate status.
- Group 04 control-effect claims remain under these rules. Observed gate or safeguard state does not by itself prove a broader downstream consequence was caused by that control.
- REF-028 provides a narrow prevented-exposure standard for materially operative safeguard enforcement; broader consequence attribution remains Causal Claim.
- Causal conclusions about gate/safeguard effects cannot outrun enforcement evidence.
- Confirmed claims remain challengeable; later evidence may revise current status while preserving historical confirmation and its evidence cut/standard/provenance.
- Explanations must preserve exact causal epistemic status.

Do not begin Phase 004 Group 05 without explicit user request.
